1
00:00:00,500 --> 00:00:03,800
part 1 chapter 3.

2
00:00:03,800 --> 00:00:06,980
Winston was dreaming of his mother.

3
00:00:06,980 --> 00:00:11,838
He must, he thought of been 10 or 11 years old when his mother had disappeared.

4
00:00:11,838 --> 00:00:18,380
She was a tall, statuesque, rather silent woman with slow movements and magnificent fair hair.

5
00:00:18,380 --> 00:00:24,619
His father he remembered more vaguely as dark and thin, dressed always in neat dark clothes

6
00:00:24,619 --> 00:00:29,000
(Winston remembered especially the very thin soles of his father's shoes) and

7
00:00:29,000 --> 00:00:30,858
wearing spectacles.

8
00:00:30,858 --> 00:00:34,400
The two of them must evidently have been swallowed up in one of the first great

9
00:00:34,400 --> 00:00:39,019
purges of the 50s At this moment his mother was sitting in

10
00:00:39,019 --> 00:00:44,179
some place deep down beneath him, with his young sister in her arms.

11
00:00:44,179 --> 00:00:48,920
He did not remember his sister at all, except as a tiny, feeble baby, always

12
00:00:48,920 --> 00:00:52,099
silent, with large, watchful eyes.

13
00:00:52,099 --> 00:00:54,500
Both of them were looking up at him.

14
00:00:54,500 --> 00:00:58,639
They were down in some subterranean place— —the bottom of a well, for instance,

15
00:00:58,639 --> 00:01:03,139
or a very deep grave— —but it was a place which, already far

16
00:01:03,139 --> 00:01:06,380
below him, was itself moving downwards.

17
00:01:06,380 --> 00:01:11,780
They were in the saloon of a sinking ship, looking up at him through the darkening water.

18
00:01:11,780 --> 00:01:16,519
There was still air in the saloon, they could still see him and he them, but all

19
00:01:16,519 --> 00:01:20,480
the while they were sinking down, down into the green waters which in another

20
00:01:20,480 --> 00:01:25,699
moment must hide them from sight forever He was out in the light and air while

21
00:01:25,699 --> 00:01:30,980
they were being sucked down to death, and they were down there because he was up here.

22
00:01:30,980 --> 00:01:35,599
He knew it and they knew it, and he could see the knowledge in their faces. There

23
00:01:35,599 --> 00:01:38,780
was no reproach either in their faces or in their hearts,

24
00:01:38,780 --> 00:01:43,280
only the knowledge that they must die in order that he might remain alive, and

25
00:01:43,280 --> 00:01:47,420
that this was part of the unavoidable order of things.

26
00:01:47,420 --> 00:01:51,079
He could not remember what had happened, but he knew in his dream that in some

27
00:01:51,079 --> 00:01:55,760
way the lives of his mother and his sister had been sacrificed to his own.

28
00:01:55,760 --> 00:01:59,780
It was one of those dreams which, while retaining the characteristic dream

29
00:01:59,780 --> 00:02:04,039
scenery, are a continuation of one's intellectual life, and in which one

30
00:02:04,039 --> 00:02:09,858
becomes aware of facts and ideas which still seem new and valuable after one is awake.

31
00:02:09,858 --> 00:02:13,699
The thing that now suddenly struck Winston was that his mother's death,

32
00:02:13,699 --> 00:02:20,239
nearly 30 years ago, had been tragic and sorrowful in a way that was no longer possible.

33
00:02:20,239 --> 00:02:24,559
Tragedy, he perceived, belonged to the ancient time, to a time when there was

34
00:02:24,559 --> 00:02:28,940
still privacy, love and friendship, and when the members of a family stood by

35
00:02:28,940 --> 00:02:31,880
one another without needing to know the reason.

36
00:02:31,880 --> 00:02:36,739
His mother's memory tore at his heart because she had died loving him, when he

37
00:02:36,739 --> 00:02:41,000
was too young and selfish to love her in return, and because somehow, he did not

38
00:02:41,000 --> 00:02:45,800
remember how, she had sacrificed herself to a conception of loyalty that was

39
00:02:45,800 --> 00:02:48,619
private and unalterable.

40
00:02:48,619 --> 00:02:53,480
Such things, he saw, could not happen today. Today there were fear, hatred and

41
00:02:53,480 --> 00:02:58,519
pain, but no dignity of emotion, no deep or complex sorrows.

42
00:02:58,519 --> 00:03:02,179
All this he seemed to see in the large eyes of his mother and his sister,

43
00:03:02,179 --> 00:03:08,358
looking up at him through the green water, hundreds of fathoms down and still sinking.

44
00:03:08,358 --> 00:03:13,099
Suddenly he was standing on short springy turf, on a summer evening when

45
00:03:13,099 --> 00:03:16,159
the slanting rays of the sun gilded the ground.

46
00:03:16,159 --> 00:03:20,358
The landscape that he was looking at recurred so often in his dreams that he

47
00:03:20,358 --> 00:03:24,559
was never fully certain whether or not he had seen it in the real world.

48
00:03:24,559 --> 00:03:27,980
In his waking thoughts he called it the Golden Country.

49
00:03:27,980 --> 00:03:32,659
It was an old rabbit bitten pasture, with a foot track wandering across it and a

50
00:03:32,659 --> 00:03:35,059
molehill here and there.

51
00:03:35,059 --> 00:03:39,199
In the ragged hedge on the opposite side of the field the bowels of the elm trees

52
00:03:39,199 --> 00:03:43,579
were swaying very faintly in the breeze, their leaves just stirring in dense

53
00:03:43,579 --> 00:03:46,059
masses like women's hair.

54
00:03:46,059 --> 00:03:50,599
Somewhere near at hand, though out of sight, there was a clear, slow-moving

55
00:03:50,599 --> 00:03:56,199
stream where dace was swimming in the pools under the willow trees.

56
00:03:56,420 --> 00:04:00,559
The girl with dark hair was coming towards them across the field.

57
00:04:00,559 --> 00:04:06,440
With what seemed a single movement she tore off her clothes and flung them disdainfully aside.

58
00:04:06,440 --> 00:04:10,818
Her body was white and smooth, but it aroused no desire in him, indeed he

59
00:04:10,818 --> 00:04:15,260
barely looked at it. What overwhelmed him in that instant was admiration for the

60
00:04:15,260 --> 00:04:18,619
gesture with which she had thrown her clothes aside.

61
00:04:18,619 --> 00:04:23,059
With its grace and carelessness it seemed to annihilate a whole culture, a

62
00:04:23,059 --> 00:04:26,659
whole system of thought, as though Big Brother and the Party and the Thought

63
00:04:26,659 --> 00:04:32,179
Police could all be swept into nothingness by a single splendid movement of the arm.

64
00:04:32,179 --> 00:04:35,480
That too was a gesture belonging to the ancient time.

65
00:04:35,480 --> 00:04:40,659
Winston woke up with the word 'Shakespeare' on his lips.

66
00:04:40,760 --> 00:04:44,659
The telescreen was giving forth an ear splitting whistle which continued on the

67
00:04:44,659 --> 00:04:47,000
same note for 30 seconds.

68
00:04:47,000 --> 00:04:51,559
It was North 715 getting up time for office workers.

69
00:04:51,559 --> 00:04:56,420
Winston wrenched his body out of bed— —naked, for a member of the Outer Party

70
00:04:56,420 --> 00:05:01,760
received only 3 000 clothing coupons annually, and a suit of pajamas was 600.

71
00:05:01,760 --> 00:05:07,099
and seized the dingy singlet and a pair of shorts that were lying across a chair.

72
00:05:07,099 --> 00:05:10,099
The Physical jokes would begin in three minutes.

73
00:05:10,099 --> 00:05:13,699
The next moment he was doubled up by a violent coughing fit which nearly always

74
00:05:13,699 --> 00:05:18,199
attacked him soon after waking up. It emptied his lungs so completely that he

75
00:05:18,199 --> 00:05:23,480
could only begin breathing again by lying on his back and taking a series of deep gasps.

76
00:05:23,480 --> 00:05:30,139
His veins had swelled with the effort of the cough, and the varicus also had started itching

77
00:05:30,139 --> 00:05:35,960
30 to 40 group!' yapped a piercing female voice 30 to 40 group! Take your places,

78
00:05:35,960 --> 00:05:41,000
please 30s to 40s Winston sprang to a tension in front of

79
00:05:41,000 --> 00:05:45,318
the telescreen, upon which the image of a youngish woman, scrawny but muscular,

80
00:05:45,318 --> 00:05:49,699
dressed in tunic and gym shoes had already appeared.

81
00:05:49,699 --> 00:05:55,760
'Arms bending and stretching!' she wrapped out. 'Take your time by me. One, two, three,

82
00:05:55,760 --> 00:06:02,300
four! One, two, three, four! Come on, comrades, put a bit of life into it! One, two, three,

83
00:06:02,300 --> 00:06:05,800
four! One, two, three, four!...'

84
00:06:05,800 --> 00:06:09,980
The pain of the coughing fitted not quite driven out of Winston's mind the

85
00:06:09,980 --> 00:06:15,559
impression made by his dream, and the rhythmic movements of the exercise restored it somewhat.

86
00:06:15,559 --> 00:06:19,579
As he mechanically shot his arms back and forth, wearing on his face the look

87
00:06:19,579 --> 00:06:23,780
of grim enjoyment which was considered proper during the Physical Jerks, he was

88
00:06:23,780 --> 00:06:28,460
struggling to think his way backward into the dim period of his early childhood.

89
00:06:28,460 --> 00:06:31,039
It was extraordinarily difficult.

90
00:06:31,039 --> 00:06:34,400
Beyond the late 50s everything faded.

91
00:06:34,400 --> 00:06:38,119
When there were no external records that you could refer to, even the outline of

92
00:06:38,119 --> 00:06:40,519
your own life lost its sharpness.

93
00:06:40,519 --> 00:06:44,239
You remembered huge events which had quite probably not happened, you

94
00:06:44,239 --> 00:06:47,420
remembered the detail of incidents without being able to recapture their

95
00:06:47,420 --> 00:06:52,280
atmosphere, and there were long blank periods to which you could assign nothing

96
00:06:52,280 --> 00:06:54,380
had been different then.

97
00:06:54,380 --> 00:06:58,639
Even the names of countries, and their shapes on the map, had been different.

98
00:06:58,639 --> 00:07:03,199
Airstrip One, for instance, had not been so called in those days: it had been

99
00:07:03,199 --> 00:07:09,980
called England or Britain, though London, he felt fairly certain, had always been called London.

100
00:07:09,980 --> 00:07:13,760
Winston could not definitely remember a time when his country had not been at

101
00:07:13,760 --> 00:07:17,420
war, but it was evident that there had been a fairly long interval of peace

102
00:07:17,420 --> 00:07:21,500
during his childhood, because one of his early memories was of an air raid which

103
00:07:21,500 --> 00:07:24,260
appeared to take everyone by surprise.

104
00:07:24,260 --> 00:07:28,699
Perhaps it was the time when the atomic bomb had fallen on Colchester.

105
00:07:28,699 --> 00:07:32,239
He did not remember the raid itself, but he did remember his father's hand

106
00:07:32,239 --> 00:07:37,280
clutching his own as they hurried down, down, down into some place deep in the

107
00:07:37,280 --> 00:07:41,900
earth, round and round a spiral staircase which rang under his feet and which

108
00:07:41,900 --> 00:07:47,539
finally so wearied his legs that he began whimpering and they had to stop and rest.

109
00:07:47,539 --> 00:07:52,099
His mother, in her slow dreamy way, was following a long way behind them.

110
00:07:52,099 --> 00:07:56,599
She was carrying his baby's sister— —or perhaps it was only a bundle of blankets

111
00:07:56,599 --> 00:08:01,579
that she was carrying: he was not certain whether his sister had been born then.

112
00:08:01,579 --> 00:08:08,838
Finally they had emerged into a noisy, crowded place which he had realized to be a Tube station.

113
00:08:08,838 --> 00:08:12,619
There were people sitting all over the stone flagged floor, and other people,

114
00:08:12,619 --> 00:08:17,358
packed tightly together, were sitting on metal bunks, one above the other.

115
00:08:17,358 --> 00:08:22,039
Winston and his mother and father found themselves a place on the floor, and near

116
00:08:22,039 --> 00:08:26,420
them an old man and an old woman was sitting side by side on a bunk.

117
00:08:26,420 --> 00:08:31,039
The old man had on a decent dark suit and a black cloth cap pushed back from

118
00:08:31,039 --> 00:08:34,579
very white hair: his face was scarlet and his eyes were

119
00:08:34,579 --> 00:08:40,159
blue and full of tears. He reeked of Jinn It seemed to breathe out of his skin in

120
00:08:40,159 --> 00:08:45,679
place of sweat, and one could have fancied that the tears wailing from his eyes were pure gin.

121
00:08:45,679 --> 00:08:51,440
But those slightly drunk he was also suffering under some grief that was genuine and unbearable.

122
00:08:51,440 --> 00:08:56,119
In his childish way Winston grasped that some terrible thing, something that was

123
00:08:56,119 --> 00:09:00,199
beyond forgiveness and could never be remedied, had just happened.

124
00:09:00,199 --> 00:09:03,440
It also seemed to him that he knew what it was.

125
00:09:03,440 --> 00:09:08,780
Someone whom the old man loved, a little granddaughter perhaps, had been killed.

126
00:09:08,780 --> 00:09:14,659
Every few minutes the old man kept repeating: 'We didn't ought to have trusted him I

127
00:09:14,659 --> 00:09:16,519
said so, Ma, didn't I?

128
00:09:16,519 --> 00:09:20,780
That's what comes of trusting him I said so all along.

129
00:09:20,780 --> 00:09:24,260
We didn't ought to have trusted the buggers.'

130
00:09:24,260 --> 00:09:29,420
But which buggers they didn't ought to have trusted Winston could not now remember.

131
00:09:29,420 --> 00:09:33,380
Since about that time, war had been literally continuous so strictly

132
00:09:33,380 --> 00:09:36,199
speaking it had not always been the same war.

133
00:09:36,199 --> 00:09:40,159
For several months during his childhood that had been confused street fighting

134
00:09:40,159 --> 00:09:43,639
in London itself, some of which he remembered vividly.

135
00:09:43,639 --> 00:09:47,119
But to trace out the history of the whole period, to say who was fighting

136
00:09:47,119 --> 00:09:51,260
whom at any given moment, would have been utterly impossible, since no written

137
00:09:51,260 --> 00:09:58,099
record, and no spoken word, ever made mention of any other alignment than the existing one.

138
00:09:58,099 --> 00:10:04,760
At this moment, for example, in 1984 (if it was 1984), Oceania was at war with Eurasia

139
00:10:04,760 --> 00:10:09,679
and in alliance with East Asia In no public or private utterance was it

140
00:10:09,679 --> 00:10:14,239
ever admitted that the three powers had at any time been grouped along different lines.

141
00:10:14,239 --> 00:10:18,440
Actually, as Winston well knew, it was only four years since Oceania had been

142
00:10:18,440 --> 00:10:21,858
at war with East Asia and in alliance with Eurasia.

143
00:10:21,858 --> 00:10:25,280
But that was merely a piece of furtive knowledge which he happened to possess

144
00:10:25,280 --> 00:10:29,298
because his memory was not satisfactorily under control.

145
00:10:29,298 --> 00:10:32,358
Officially the change of partners had never happened.

146
00:10:32,358 --> 00:10:38,000
Oceania was at war with Eurasia: therefore Oceania had always been at war with Eurasia.

147
00:10:38,000 --> 00:10:41,659
The enemy of the moment always represented absolute evil, and it

148
00:10:41,659 --> 00:10:46,338
followed that any past or future agreement with him was impossible.

149
00:10:46,338 --> 00:10:50,780
The frightening thing, he reflected for the ten thousandth time as he forced his

150
00:10:50,780 --> 00:10:55,219
shoulders painfully backward (with hands on hips, they were gyrating their bodies

151
00:10:55,219 --> 00:10:59,239
from the waist, an exercise that was supposed to be good for the back muscles)—

152
00:10:59,239 --> 00:11:02,659
)—the frightening thing was that it might all be true.

153
00:11:02,659 --> 00:11:06,338
If the Party could thrust its hand into the past and say of this or that event,

154
00:11:06,338 --> 00:11:13,400
it never happened— —that, surely, was more terrifying than mere torture and death?

155
00:11:13,400 --> 00:11:18,199
The Party said that Oceania had never been in alliance with Eurasia.

156
00:11:18,199 --> 00:11:23,000
He, Winston Smith, knew that Oceania had been an alliance with Eurasia as shorter

157
00:11:23,000 --> 00:11:24,920
time as four years ago.

158
00:11:24,920 --> 00:11:27,199
But where did that knowledge exist?

159
00:11:27,199 --> 00:11:32,179
Only in his own consciousness, which in any case must soon be annihilated.

160
00:11:32,179 --> 00:11:37,099
And if all others accepted the lie which the part he imposed— —if all records told

161
00:11:37,099 --> 00:11:42,260
the same tale— —then the lie passed into history and became truth.

162
00:11:42,260 --> 00:11:47,239
'Who controls the past,' ran the Party slogan, 'controls the future: who controls

163
00:11:47,239 --> 00:11:49,818
the present controls the past.'

164
00:11:49,818 --> 00:11:54,980
And yet the past, though of its nature alterable, never had been altered.

165
00:11:54,980 --> 00:11:59,059
Whatever was true now was true from everlasting to everlasting.

166
00:11:59,059 --> 00:12:00,980
It was quite simple.

167
00:12:00,980 --> 00:12:05,539
All that was needed was an unending series of victories over your own memory.

168
00:12:05,539 --> 00:12:10,838
'Reality control', they called it: in Newspeak, 'doublethink'.

169
00:12:10,838 --> 00:12:16,280
'Stand easy!' barked the instructors a little more genially.

170
00:12:16,280 --> 00:12:21,019
Winston sank his arms to his sides and slowly refilled his lungs with air.

171
00:12:21,019 --> 00:12:25,219
His mind slid away into the labyrinthine world of doublethink.

172
00:12:25,219 --> 00:12:29,659
To know and not to know, to be conscious of complete truthfulness while telling

173
00:12:29,659 --> 00:12:33,798
carefully constructed lies, to hold simultaneously two opinions which

174
00:12:33,798 --> 00:12:38,179
canceled out, knowing them to be contradictory and believing in both of them;

175
00:12:38,179 --> 00:12:43,460
to use logic against logic, to repudiate morality while laying claim to it, to

176
00:12:43,460 --> 00:12:46,639
believe that the democracy was impossible and that the Party was the

177
00:12:46,639 --> 00:12:51,500
guardian of democracy; to forget whatever it was necessary to forget, then to draw

178
00:12:51,500 --> 00:12:55,400
it back into memory again at the moment when it was needed, and then promptly to

179
00:12:55,400 --> 00:13:01,159
forget it again: and above all, to apply the same process to the process itself.

180
00:13:01,159 --> 00:13:05,119
That was the ultimate subtlety: consciously to induce unconsciousness,

181
00:13:05,119 --> 00:13:11,000
and then, once again, to become unconscious of the act of hypnosis you had just performed.

182
00:13:11,000 --> 00:13:15,980
Even to understand the word double think involved the use of doublethink.

183
00:13:15,980 --> 00:13:19,280
The instructors had called them to attention again.

184
00:13:19,280 --> 00:13:24,019
'And now let's see which of us can touch our toes!' she said enthusiastically. 'Right

185
00:13:24,019 --> 00:13:32,539
over from the hips, please, comrades one two one do Winston loathed this exercise, which sent

186
00:13:32,539 --> 00:13:36,380
shooting pains all the way from his heels to his buttocks and often ended by

187
00:13:36,380 --> 00:13:38,539
bringing on another coughing fit.

188
00:13:38,539 --> 00:13:42,019
The half Pleasant quality went out of his meditations.

189
00:13:42,019 --> 00:13:47,298
The past, he reflected, had not merely been altered, it had actually been destroyed.

190
00:13:47,298 --> 00:13:51,318
For how could you establish even the most obvious fact when there existed no

191
00:13:51,318 --> 00:13:54,019
record outside your own memory?

192
00:13:54,019 --> 00:13:58,579
He tried to remember in what year he had first heard mention of Big Brother.

193
00:13:58,579 --> 00:14:02,358
He thought it must have been at some time in the 60s but it was impossible to

194
00:14:02,358 --> 00:14:03,318
be certain.

195
00:14:03,318 --> 00:14:06,980
In the Party histories, of course, Big Brother figured as the leader and

196
00:14:06,980 --> 00:14:10,579
guardian of the Revolution since its very earliest days.

197
00:14:10,579 --> 00:14:14,780
His exploits had been gradually pushed backwards in time until already they

198
00:14:14,780 --> 00:14:19,760
extended into the fabulous world of the 40s and the 30s when the capitalists in

199
00:14:19,760 --> 00:14:23,659
their strange cylindrical hats still rode through the streets of London in

200
00:14:23,659 --> 00:14:28,099
great gleaming Motorcars or horse carriages with glass sides.

201
00:14:28,099 --> 00:14:32,420
There was no knowing how much of this legend was true and how much invented.

202
00:14:32,420 --> 00:14:37,219
Winston could not even remember at what date the Party itself had come into existence.

203
00:14:37,219 --> 00:14:42,019
He did not believe he had ever heard the word insock before 1960.

204
00:14:42,019 --> 00:14:47,480
it was possible that in its old speak form—' —'English Socialism', that is to say— —it

205
00:14:47,480 --> 00:14:49,099
had been current earlier.

206
00:14:49,099 --> 00:14:51,380
Everything melted into mist.

207
00:14:51,380 --> 00:14:55,940
Sometimes indeed, you could put your finger on a definite lie. It was not true,

208
00:14:55,940 --> 00:15:01,400
for example, as was claimed in the Party history books, that the Party had invented airplanes

209
00:15:01,400 --> 00:15:04,639
He remembered airplanes since his earliest childhood.

210
00:15:04,639 --> 00:15:06,380
But you could prove nothing.

211
00:15:06,380 --> 00:15:08,420
There was never any evidence.

212
00:15:08,420 --> 00:15:13,219
Just once in his whole life he had held in his hands unmistakable documentary

213
00:15:13,219 --> 00:15:16,880
proof of the falsification of an historical fact.

214
00:15:16,880 --> 00:15:23,199
And on that occasion— 'Smith!' screamed the shrewish voice from the telescreen.

215
00:15:23,199 --> 00:15:28,159
'6079 Smith W! Yes, you! Bend lower, please!

216
00:15:28,159 --> 00:15:32,719
You can do better than that. You're not trying. Lower, please!

217
00:15:32,719 --> 00:15:34,818
That's better, comrade.

218
00:15:34,818 --> 00:15:39,440
Now stand at ease, the whole squad, and watch me.'

219
00:15:39,440 --> 00:15:43,039
A sudden hot sweat had broken out all over Winston's body.

220
00:15:43,039 --> 00:15:45,980
His face remains completely inscrutable.

221
00:15:45,980 --> 00:15:48,798
Never show dismay! Never show resentment!

222
00:15:48,798 --> 00:15:52,280
A single flicker of the eyes could give you away.

223
00:15:52,280 --> 00:15:56,599
He stood watching while the instructors raised her arms above her head and— —one

224
00:15:56,599 --> 00:16:00,619
could not say gracefully, but with remarkable neatness and efficiency— —bent

225
00:16:00,619 --> 00:16:04,940
over and tucked the first joint of her fingers under her toes.

226
00:16:04,940 --> 00:16:11,179
'There, comrades! That's how I want to see you doing it. Watch me again. I'm 39 and

227
00:16:11,179 --> 00:16:12,798
I've had four children.

228
00:16:12,798 --> 00:16:14,358
Now look.'

229
00:16:14,358 --> 00:16:19,639
She bent over again. 'You see my knees aren't bent. You can all do it if you

230
00:16:19,639 --> 00:16:24,500
want to,' she added as she straightened herself up. 'Anyone under 45 is perfectly

231
00:16:24,500 --> 00:16:26,539
capable of touching his toes.

232
00:16:26,539 --> 00:16:30,020
We don't all have the privilege of fighting in the front line, but at least

233
00:16:30,020 --> 00:16:31,759
we can all keep fit.

234
00:16:31,759 --> 00:16:34,460
Remember our boys on the Malabar front!

235
00:16:34,460 --> 00:16:38,359
And the sailors in the Floating Fortresses! Just think what they have to

236
00:16:38,359 --> 00:16:44,000
put up with. Now try again. That's better comrade, that's much better,' she added

237
00:16:44,000 --> 00:16:48,738
encouragingly as Winston, with a violent lunge, succeeded in touching his toes

238
00:16:48,738 --> 00:16:53,000
with knees unbent, for the first time in several years
